Score: 6.2/10
Verdict: almost
Weaknesses (ranked, with 1-sentence minimum fix per item): 1) L2 is not cleanly defined as “dev oracle only”: §5 and Table 5 present L2 as a standalone dev-label rulebook, but the L2 protocol and script both require the L1 rulebook as input, so the L2 row is contaminated by ref-derived knowledge and is not comparable to L1+L2; minimum fix: either redefine L2 as “L2 conditioned on L1 context but injects only L2 rules” and add a true no-L1 L2 ablation, or remove L1 from the L2 reflector input and implementation. 2) The L2 corrective-rule logic is underspecified for high-uncertainty items that Passive v9 gets correct: the text says anomaly extensions are for missed anomalies and tolerances for over-flagged normals, but uncertainty sampling will select many correctly classified borderline items and the template still forces exactly one “corrective” rule; minimum fix: explicitly define rules for correct-but-uncertain dev items, allow “no rule” or calibration/tolerance rules, and report how many selected items are actual errors. 3) The per-domain oracle budget conflicts with per-class rulebook granularity: K=10 labels per domain is sparse for D1/D3/D6 with many classes, so many per-class L2 rulebooks may receive zero corrective evidence while others get multiple rules; minimum fix: report selected-dev class coverage and define the fallback behavior for classes without L2 rules, or change selection to class-stratified K while keeping the oracle budget explicit. 4) The implementation fidelity is currently weaker than the methodological claims: L1 says “sample” refs but the shown code uses the first n refs, and it does not enforce the claimed strict schema, 3-to-6 list lengths, or nonempty anomaly modes beyond key presence; minimum fix: make ref sampling seeded and random, validate JSON fields and cardinalities, and retry/reject malformed or underspecified rulebooks. 5) The holdout protocol is conceptually useful but currently not enough to validate rulebook quality: dev-holdout AUROC is measured after the selected 10 items influenced the rulebook, yet there is no nested model-selection boundary, confidence interval, or rule-length/cost normalization, so it may still overstate robustness; minimum fix: pre-register that holdout is diagnostic only, report per-domain paired deltas with uncertainty, and include rulebook token/call cost in the same comparison. 6) The motivation overclaims super-additivity before evidence: the L1+L2 paragraph says the stack “therefore tests” complementarity, but the current setup lacks an interaction test separating L1, L2, and L1-conditioned-L2; minimum fix: add a factorial comparison or state the complementarity as a hypothesis, not an implied design consequence. 7) §5 appears consistent with deleting old §4.7 only if all references to `ssec:active_learning` were removed, but the provided text still references the pre-review active-learning appendix and `al_pilot_appendix`; minimum fix: verify no stale `ssec:active_learning` cross-reference remains and make the appendix citation clearly historical rather than a live method dependency.
Memory update for R4: Focus first on whether L2 has been disentangled from L1, whether correct-but-uncertain dev items and class coverage are reported, and whether the implementation now enforces seeded sampling plus rulebook schema validation; accept t.b.d. numerical cells only if the protocol definitions are now internally consistent.
